<!--
    Theme-independent shared resources: the window icon and named styles that are not control chrome.

    These live in a merged dictionary rather than directly in Application.Resources so they are
    addressable by pack:// URI. That matters for anything that composes the resource set without going
    through App.xaml - the UI smoke harness does exactly that, and resources declared inline in
    Application.Resources are invisible to it, which shows up as "Cannot find resource named …" at the
    first control that needs one.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        The application mark: title-bar and Alt+Tab icon for every window, and the image in the About
        window. Theme-independent, and so is the notification-area icon, which is now this same file -
        a coloured tile reads against a light or a dark background alike, which is what retired the pair
        of monochrome tray glyphs this used to be contrasted with.

        Note the tray cannot use this entry: it needs a filesystem path for LoadImage, and a pack:// URI
        is not one. See the Content item in PasteJump.App.csproj.
    -->
    <!--
        DecodePixelWidth is not optional, and leaving it off was a real bug: a BitmapImage decodes exactly one
        frame of an .ico, and with no requested size WPF's icon decoder picks the SMALLEST - measured, 16 of the
        nine frames present. Window.Icon then has nothing but that 16px bitmap to answer every request with, so
        the taskbar button, Alt+Tab and Task Manager - which ask for 32 or more - all rendered a 16px image,
        visibly smaller than every neighbouring icon rather than merely soft.

        32 rather than 256, and that correction was itself reported: asking for the largest frame fixed the size
        and made the taskbar icon blurry instead, because ~8:1 is a brutal reduction for a mark with a 1px rim
        and a one-pixel gap between its cards. "Downscaling beats upscaling" holds for photographs; for a
        pixel-tuned icon the hand-authored frame at the requested size beats any resampling of a bigger one,
        which is the entire reason multi-frame icons exist.

        32 is what the taskbar and Alt+Tab ask for - SM_CXICON - at 100% scaling, so at that scale the frame is
        used as authored with no resampling at all, and the 16px title bar is a 2:1 reduction within the same
        family. The honest limitation: at 150% the taskbar wants 48 and will upscale from 32, so this is tuned
        for the common case rather than universally right. The better fix is to drop Window.Icon entirely and
        let Windows choose from all nine frames in the PE header, which is what Explorer already does - not
        taken here only because no screenshot can verify a taskbar button, and a wrong guess means windows with
        no icon at all rather than a soft one.
    -->
    <BitmapImage x:Key="AppIcon"
                 UriSource="pack://application:,,,/PasteJump;component/Assets/pastejump.ico"
                 DecodePixelWidth="32"
                 DecodePixelHeight="32" />

    <!--
        The mark for anything that *displays* it rather than using it as a window icon.

        A separate resource, and a PNG rather than the .ico, because a multi-frame icon is the wrong source
        for a chosen render size: WPF's icon decoder picks a frame itself, and given no requested decode size
        it can pick a small one and scale it up. That is exactly what made the About window's 48px image look
        soft - it was a 32px frame enlarged. One frame at 256 removes the choice, and downscaling is always
        cleaner than the reverse.

        Generated by tools/generate-icon.ps1 -PngPath, so it cannot drift from the icon.
    -->
    <BitmapImage x:Key="AppIconLarge" UriSource="pack://application:,,,/PasteJump;component/Assets/pastejump-256.png" />

    <!-- Small muted label used for the overlay's status chips. -->
    <Style x:Key="ChipText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource MutedTextBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
